 "overview": {
  "summary": "The instrument's high voltage workhorse. A16 outputs dc from ±22 V to ±219.99999 V and ac from 22 V to 219.99999 V rms, and it drives the High Voltage assemblies A14 and A15 in every high voltage and high current function -- so a genuine A16 fault takes out the 220 V ranges, the 1100 V ranges and the 2.2 A range together. It is a single feedback loop built in three stages: a transconductance input stage around heated hybrid HR8, a voltage-gain mid stage on two 800 V MOSFETs, and an emitter-follower output stage stacked two devices deep on each side to stand off the ±PA rails. All the voltage gain comes from the first two stages; the third only supplies current. Gain is set by which resistors the relays select -- -20 in dc from the 500 kΩ/25 kΩ network on HR8, -10 in ac from R11+R12+R13 over R17 -- so most 'wrong output' faults are a relay or its drive rather than an amplifier fault. Around the loop sit three support circuits that generate most of the fault codes: sense-current cancellation, the 220 V range ac attenuator, and the 220 V dc internal calibration network.",
  "sections": "§2-139 … §2-154",
  "note": "The ±PA supplies come from A18 and reach ±365 V; the output reaches 220 V rms. Troubleshooting means removing the rear high voltage shield (MP50) and the front air duct (MP58) and running the board live on the extender card, with the ±PA rail bypass capacitors C27, C78 and C79 -- 3.3 µF at 450 V -- charged. §5-22 step 3 exists precisely so the main loop can be worked on at ±60 V instead: use it."
 },
